\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Comparing energies when elimination is singular}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{The real Schur calculation in \ref{fdip-001Y} eliminates a
strictly positive component by inverting it. A nonnegative quadratic
form can instead have an eliminated direction of zero energy, for
which that inverse is unavailable. Fix a finite-dimensional vector
space over an ordered field and a positive symmetric pairing.
How can we compare its quadratic energies and describe what remains
on a chosen subspace?}
